+The radial Gauss map associates a radial direction with an outer
+normal on the boundary of a convex body. Its role in the Gauss image
+problem is studied by B\"or\"oczky, Lutwak, Yang, Zhang and Zhao
+\citep{boroczky2020gaussimage}. Our functional features this same
+radial--normal correspondence, but the basepoint $p$ varies inside the
+body. For comparison, Bertrand's optimal-transport formulation of
+Gauss-curvature prescription uses (in his Eq.~(6)) the angular cost
+$c(n,u)=-\log\cos d_{\mathbb S^2}(n,u)$ between an outward normal
+$n$ and a radial direction $u$ \citep{bertrand2016gauss}. Here the
+cost is instead the squared angle $d_{\mathbb S^2}(n,u)^2$, averaged
+against cone-volume probability; we do not invoke the Gauss image
+or curvature-prescription theorems to classify its stationary points.
+
